---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Screenshot from "../components/Screenshot.astro";
import UiLabel from "../components/UiLabel.astro";
import { WEB_APP_HOST, WEB_APP_URL, webAppIsLive } from "../lib/web-app.mjs";

// Issue #1160: the marketing home. The family-collaboration story leads
// (docs/product/positioning.md) — track a cycle together, guardians by
// role, reminders that reach the right person, estimates that explain
// themselves — told with the real app (the #1104 scripted screenshots),
// not abstractions. Privacy is one calm supporting section, never the
// headline; no security-speak in the hero. Every factual claim carries a
// `cite:` comment and a ledger entry in site/claims.md, which
// test/site/site_claims_test.dart checks against the repo. Both sharing
// directions get equal weight, and the age line matches PRIVACY.md §5.

const tagline =
  "A cycle tracker a family shares: one profile, every entry attributed " +
  "to who logged it, estimates that explain themselves, and reminders " +
  "that reach the right person.";

// The deploy tells the build whether the address is serving the browser
// version right now (src/lib/web-app.mjs). When it is not, the button
// would lead to an error page, so the hero says so instead.
const browserUrl = WEB_APP_URL;
const browserLive = webAppIsLive();
---

<BaseLayout title="lunarlog" description={tagline}>
  {
    /* The words and the product open the page together: side by side on
       a wide window, one above the other on a narrow one (global.css,
       .hero-band). */
  }
  <div class="hero-band">
    <section class="hero">
      <h1 class="hero__title">Track a cycle, together.</h1>
      <p class="hero__tagline">{tagline}</p>
      {
        browserLive ? (
          <a class="button" href={browserUrl}>Use lunarlog in your browser</a>
        ) : (
          <>
            <p class="hero__notice">
              The browser version is not available right now. It will be
              back at {WEB_APP_HOST}.
            </p>
            <a class="button" href="/guides/">Read the guides</a>
          </>
        )
      }
      <p class="hero__fineprint">Free for personal use.</p>
    </section>

    <figure class="screenshot-figure screenshot-figure--hero">
      <Screenshot
        screen="today"
        device="iphone-67"
        theme="light"
        loading="eager"
        alt="The lunarlog Today screen: the cycle day, the estimate for the next period with its confidence tier, and a card listing what was logged today."
      />
      <figcaption>
        The Today screen. The estimate is computed on the device from the
        history logged on its profile.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <!-- cite: webapp/e2e/browser-home-capture.spec.ts renders the signed-in home from webapp/e2e/fixtures.ts' fabricated snapshot (the Maya profile, no backend — issue #1431) at the #1162 browser device class, into site/public/screenshots/ at deploy time like the Flutter renders; the #1162 class' name grammar and pixel size are tool/screenshots/manifest.dart, mirrored by site/src/components/Screenshot.astro. Issue #1208 put a figure here captioned as what the button opens; the #1258 cutover made that picture the wrong app, and #1432 removed it. -->
  <figure class="screenshot-figure">
    <Screenshot
      screen="today"
      device="browser"
      theme="light"
      loading="lazy"
      alt="The browser version's signed-in home: the profile's cycle day and estimate, a card of what was logged today, and the shared month calendar."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The browser version's home — the React web client that "Use lunarlog
      in your browser" opens, captured from the same fabricated data its
      own tests run on.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Track a cycle together</h2>
    <!-- cite: docs/product/positioning.md "The family-collaboration story"; PRIVACY.md §1 "Built For Sync & Family Collaboration"; §3 "Cross-Device Synchronization"; §2.A "Same-date merge disclosures"; §5 attribution; the calendar's labelled estimate bands per lib/l10n/app_en.arb (calendarCellPredictedPeriod, calendarCellPmsWindow). -->
    <p>
      One profile holds one cycle, and it is shared, not siloed: what one
      guardian logs, the profile's other guardians see on the same
      calendar. Every entry carries the name of the person who logged it,
      and when two guardians log the same day without knowing, lunarlog
      merges the entries instead of overwriting one person's writing —
      and shows both of them what happened. The shared month calendar —
      what was logged beside the estimated days, always labelled as
      estimates — is on the <a href="/tracking">tracking page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Invited in by role, not all-or-nothing</h2>
    <!-- cite: one account, several profiles per PRIVACY.md §2.A "Profile information"; role names from lib/l10n/app_en.arb (guardianRoleLabel*) via UiLabel; capability facts from lib/domain/models/profile_guardian.dart; server-side enforcement per supabase/migrations/20260904010000_multi_guardian_schema.sql (RLS per role) and PRIVACY.md §5; single-use invite links in lib/domain/sharing/invite_links.dart (issue #129). -->
    <p>
      One account can keep several profiles — yours and the ones you set
      up for family members — and each profile is shared with its own
      guardians. Sharing runs on four roles —
      <UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelPrimaryGuardian" />,
      <UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelCoParent" />,
      <UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelCaregiver" />, and
      <UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelViewer" /> — so the person you invite
      gets exactly as much access as they need. Invitations are single-use
      links, you pick the role, and you can change or revoke it later. The
      server enforces every role, not just the app.
      <a href="/family-sharing">How sharing and roles work</a>.
    </p>
    <figure class="screenshot-figure">
      <Screenshot
        screen="guardians"
        device="iphone-67"
        theme="light"
        alt="The Manage guardians screen for a shared profile, listing its guardians and their roles."
      />
      <figcaption>A shared profile's guardians, each with their role.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="direction-grid">
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Her Own Profile (subject membership)"; docs/product/positioning.md "Either side can start". -->
      <div class="direction-card">
        <h3>You track for yourself</h3>
        <p>
          Start on your own device — no account needed to log. When you
          want someone alongside you, invite a parent, partner, or
          caregiver to your profile, as a reader or with hands-on access.
          You decide the role, and you can revoke it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Her Own Profile" and "Minimum-Age Policy"; issue #957. -->
      <div class="direction-card">
        <h3>You set it up for someone else</h3>
        <p>
          Create a profile for a family member and invite them to log it
          themselves from their own device. For someone under 13, that
          invitation is the only way the app begins — and accepting it is
          the record of parental consent.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Neither direction is the "real" one, and a parent holding the phone
      is never assumed to be the person whose cycle it is.
      <a href="/family-sharing">Both invitations, step by step</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Reminders that reach the right person</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §4 FCM row (opt-in per guardian; push token + profile id only; "Never a note, tag, flow level, date, or profile name"); the fixed copy is lib/domain/notifications/scheduling.dart's kReminderTitle/kReminderBody, matched character-for-character by supabase/functions/_shared/notification_copy.ts. -->
    <p>
      A guardian can turn on optional alerts — when another guardian logs
      an entry, or when an expected entry hasn't arrived — so everyone
      invited stays in the loop without asking. The alert that reaches the
      lock screen is the same fixed, generic text every time, never the
      content: no note, tag, flow level, date, or profile name ever rides
      along. <a href="/family-sharing">How alerts work</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Estimates that explain themselves</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1 "Estimates From Your Own Logged Data" (computed on the device from logged history only); confidence tiers in lib/l10n/app_en.arb (cycleConfidence*); "Estimates only — not medical advice." in lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart. -->
    <p>
      Every estimate is computed on the device, from the history the
      profile holds — nothing else — and always arrives with a confidence
      tier, so a fresh profile says so instead of guessing. They are
      estimates only, not medical advice. If something worries you, talk
      to a clinician. <a href="/tracking">What tracking looks like</a>.
    </p>
    <figure class="screenshot-figure">
      <Screenshot
        screen="estimates"
        device="iphone-67"
        theme="light"
        alt="The Today screen at the learning tier: a range estimate with its confidence framing."
      />
      <figcaption>
        A new profile's estimate says what it is: a range, at the learning
        tier.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Works when the network doesn't</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1 "Resilient Offline". -->
    <p>
      Logging, history, and estimates all run from the copy on the device
      — nothing you do offline is blocked or degraded — and what you log
      reaches the profile's guardians when a connection returns.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Your data, in and out</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 (exports, built on-device, no account required); PRIVACY.md §2.A and §4 (Clue, Apple Health, Health Connect import provenance); lib/data/import/clue_importer.dart (one-off file import — no background pass, issue #1365); lib/data/health/health_import_service.dart (importInBackground, issue #993 — the background pass is the health stores' alone and follows the completed first import, issue #1215's gate). -->
    <p>
      Bring history in from Clue, Apple Health, or Health Connect — you
      start the first import. Apple Health and Health Connect imports
      then keep themselves current in the background once a profile is
      bound and that first import has completed; the Clue import is a
      one-off file pick. No import ever overwrites a value you logged
      by hand. For the one profile you choose, what you log is also
      written to Apple Health or Health Connect, on the phone itself.
      Take everything out, any time, as JSON, CSV, a FHIR
      summary for clinicians, or a one-page PDF: built on your device,
      with no account required.
      <a href="/import">Importing</a> ·
      <a href="/guides/moving-your-data/#health-app-writes">Your phone's health app</a> ·
      <a href="/export">Exporting</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-section">
    <h2>Private by design</h2>
    <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1 (no advertising or data brokers; scrubbed telemetry), §6 (device protection; sync is not end-to-end), §5 (minimum-age policy). -->
    <p>
      No advertising, no data brokers, no behavioral tracking. Your device
      copy sits behind its own lock. Sync runs over TLS into row-level-
      secured storage — and it is not end-to-end encrypted, which the
      privacy page states plainly along with everything else we know about
      your data. lunarlog is licensed 13 and up; someone younger uses it
      only through a parent's invitation.
      <a href="/privacy-security">Privacy and security</a> ·
      <a href="/privacy">The full policy</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <nav class="feature-grid" aria-label="Explore lunarlog">
    <a class="feature-card" href="/tracking">
      <h3>Tracking and viewing</h3>
      <p>Logging a day, reading the calendar, and what the estimates mean.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/family-sharing">
      <h3>Family sharing and roles</h3>
      <p>Invitations in both directions, and what each role can do.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/life-stage-modes">
      <h3>Life-stage modes</h3>
      <p>From first periods to postpartum — the app adapts, your data stays.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/import">
      <h3>Import</h3>
      <p>Bring history in from Clue, Apple Health, or Health Connect.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/export">
      <h3>Export</h3>
      <p>JSON, CSV, FHIR, and a clinician PDF — built on your device.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/privacy-security">
      <h3>Privacy and security</h3>
      <p>What is protected, what is shared, and what is honest about sync.</p>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <p class="doc__footer-note">
    The full privacy policy lives at <a href="/privacy">lunarlog.app/privacy</a>.
    The app's own Privacy screen summarizes it and links here.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
